ike the  speed limit says  you can drive 65 miles an hour on i-95  it's a bright-line rule if you're  traveling 66 you're violating the law  you're traveling 65 you're okay right  almost nothing in the Constitution is  written that way most of the  Constitution is written with  deliberately expansive phrases right so  like the fifth amendment protects you  from the deprivation of life liberty or  property without due process of law  right well what's Liberty what's a  process how much of it are you do all of  those are things that it's very very  clear the founding fathers intended the  judiciary to expand and explain what  those different meant so taking an  overly narrow view is is inconsistent  with the text of the Constitution and  it's also inconsistent with as far as we  can tell the writings of the founders  know right like we have the federalist  papers we know the people who wrote the  Constitution what they intended for the  judiciary to do and they did not intend  for the judiciary to fetishize either  the text of the Constitution or the  writings of the founding club right so  if you want to look back to what the  founding fathers thought they didn't  think hey put us on a pedestal and never  change stuff they thought we're giving  you a framework that contains fanciful  firms and we want that to fill out over  time and they're there a number of you  know sort of really clear examples of  this where originalism just gets you you  know absurd results can you give us a  couple of those examples I think that'd  be interesting yeah sure so for example  the Eighth Amendment protects  individuals against cruel and unusual  punishment okay so what is cruel and  unusual punishment there have been two  different originalist takes on that  phrase okay and one is to say cruel and  unusual punishment is anything that the  founding fathers thought was cruel and  unusual punishment in 1787 the problem  with that is that's almost nothing right  in 1787 yeah it was perfectly fine to  flog people for public hangings for  pillory people in the stocks for  breaking people on the wheel like the  last law prohibiting breaking on the  wheel that is you know taking a paddle  wheel and kind of waterboarding somebody  on a sir I mean you know it's it's it's  portrait right  and the last law to prohibit that was  passed in the 20th century Inc in Rhode  Island right so so if you go by that  view then the eighth amendment literally  means nothing it does not prohibit  torture it means if you know Alabama  tomorrow decided we were gonna put  blasphemers in the stocks then they  would not have a right under the Eighth  Amendment and that's just that that  should strike you as absurd and just one  second Andrew so you're basically saying  is if you're interpreting the  Constitution in sort of this originalist  textualist way based on what the  founders meant at the time that it was  written you're talking about hey at the  time these are punishments that would  have been allowed the say textualist  interpretation would say they'd be  allowed today yeah and if I know you  have lawmakers who listen to your show  if anybody on the Senate Judiciary is  listening to the show I would love for  them to ask Neil Gorsuch what he think  what punishment specifically he thinks  the Eighth Amendment forbids is there is  no consistent originalist textualist way  to say that the Eighth Amendment forbids  anything other than you know sort of  bizarre hypothetical and I would just  ask follow-up questions say does it  prohibit public hangings does it  prohibit whipping does it prohibit  pillory does it prohibit the stock and  and there's no consistent way to answer  that question I can tell you how Antonin  Scalia answered in turn Antonin Scalia  said no no it doesn't now mind you he  didn't say that until after he'd been  confirmed by the Senate and was sitting  on the Supreme Court but that's the only  that's the only consistent view you can  have if you if y