s for Supreme Court  justices which are questions of sort of  differing specificity about abortion and  roe v wade and typically the way it goes  is individuals on the confirmation  committee will ask some kind of question  about the nominees views on abortion or  roe v-- wade and you typically get some  type of answer where it's it's this  generally a formatted answer of these  are issues that may come before the  court and it would be improper for me to  prejudge before examining the merits etc  etc etc and how did this become such a  common thing and what's the sort of  point of that at every confirmation  sure so it's important to remember roe v  wade was a seven to two decision right  like this was not a particularly I don't  wanna say it wasn't controversial it was  it was controversial politically but not  judicially because Supreme Court had  built up a body of jurisprudence  concerning what was considered the right  to privacy and privacy is sort of a  weird word for it because of the  colloquial meetings that it has but  essentially those were decisions  recognizing the key right to bodily  autonomy and to make decisions about  reproduction and childbirth and marriage  and so you had a line of decisions  starting with Griswold v Connecticut  that said states can't ban contraception  right that you had loving versus  Virginia States can't outlaw interracial  marriage and so those decisions were at  the core of what the court called the  right to privacy which in here's in the  Fourteenth Amendment and and what was  described in roe v wade a sort of a  number of the Bill of Rights is today  considered a part of of the Due Process  Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment and  so if you have the mainstream view of  jurist ress this this is the view that  it's liberal judges have it conservative  judges have it moderate judges have it  as long as you're not an originalist if  you follow sort of the mainstream idea  that precedent matters then you  recognize that there's a long standing  history of the right to privacy and then  the ROE decision is perfectly consistent  with it right you could address it you  know sort of contours at the edges like  the court did you know subsequently in  the Planned Parenthood versus Kasich  case about twenty years later but you  can't say roe v wade was wrongly decided  the only way to say that is to have kind  of a radical originalist view that says  we can bypass two hundred and twenty  years of jurisprudence and go back to  the founding of the country and say no  no we're gonna so the non answer has  come about since the four killings as a  way for conservative judicial activists  who want to reach  that end goal of overturning roe v wade  but they don't want to concede in their  hearing that that's their call they  don't want to answer squarely the  question of whether there's a right to  privacy in the Constitution because  under their philosophy there isn't but  under the judicial philosophy that again  I want to stress like 95% of lawyers of  wherever you are on the political side  of the spectrum adhere to kind of that  mainstream view that precedent matters  so that's where that come very very  informative that explains a lot about  why why we hear that so often I want to  encourage everybody to check out the  opening arguments show the legal podcast  that is co-hosted by our guest Andrew  Torres excellent program and I really  appreciate you being on with us today  Andrew Oh David thank you very much for  having me on it's been a blast

